# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The shared half of every repo's environment — COMMITTED TEMPLATE, no values.
#
#     cp env.shared .env.shared     # then fill it in, then: direnv allow
#
# `.env.shared` is gitignored (it matches `.env.*`), `env.shared` is tracked —
# the same pair as `env`/`.env` and `npmrc`/`.npmrc`. `.envrc` reads `.env.shared`
# and nothing else; see `envrc` for how the layer is loaded and what beats what.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS — ADR-053. Every key below names the SAME resource for
# every repo that reads it: one Redis cluster, one MongoDB, one CSFLE master
# key, one cookie-signing KEK, one pair of test-cluster accounts. Before this
# file each of the sixteen repos carried its own copy, so rotating one secret
# meant editing up to sixteen files and a machine where fifteen agreed and one
# did not was a *populated* misconfiguration — `RISK_REGISTER` R04, the failure
# mode where a service boots happily against the wrong server.
#
# ⚠️ A KEY BELONGS HERE ONLY IF ITS VALUE IS IDENTICAL IN EVERY REPO THAT READS
# IT. This layer is exported into the shell, and `dotenv` never overwrites an
# exported variable, so a repo's own `.env` CANNOT override anything set here.
# Adding a key that legitimately differs somewhere does not create a default —
# it creates a value that one repo can no longer change. §What stays behind
# lists the near-misses and why each one failed that test.
#
# ⚠️ AN EMPTY KEY HERE IS INERT, DELIBERATELY. `.envrc` unsets every name this
# file leaves blank, so a key you have no value for behaves as if it were not
# here at all and each repo falls back to its own `.env`. That is what makes
# this template safe to copy verbatim: an empty line cannot shadow a repo's
# working value. It also means blanking a key here does NOT blank it anywhere —
# to force an empty value on every repo, the repos are where you do it.
#
# ⚠️ A KEY CAN ALSO BE INERT BY BRANCH RATHER THAN BY BEING EMPTY. A few keys
# are read only on one branch of the platform, and a `#! active-when` /
# `#! active-unless` line above them says which — see the Redis block. Those
# lines are read by `scripts/env-shared-migrate.sh seed` and by nothing else;
# `.envrc` and `dotenv` see an ordinary comment, so the file stays safe to copy
# verbatim and loads exactly as it did before they existed.
#
# ⚠️ NAMES ONLY IN GIT. Four of the values below are secrets and this file is
# public. Fill them in `.env.shared`, never here, and never paste one into a
# commit message, an issue or a chat window.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Redis: the one cluster (10 repos) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The nine services and marketplace-services-status all address the same three
# nodes. REDIS_KEY is the namespace every key on this platform is prefixed with,
# and it is shared for a stronger reason than tidiness: two repos disagreeing on
# it means one writes sessions the other cannot read, with no error on either
# side. `assertRedisNamespace` catches that at boot; this file removes the way
# to cause it.
#
# REDIS_IS_CLUSTER is compared against exactly the string '1' by koa-utils —
# every other spelling, `true` included, is the single-node branch, silently.
#
# It is also the switch the two blocks below are read under, and the `#!` lines
# state that in a form `scripts/env-shared-migrate.sh seed` can read:
#
#     #! active-when   <KEY>=<VALUE> <NAME>…   read only while KEY holds VALUE
#     #! active-unless <KEY>=<VALUE> <NAME>…   read only while it holds anything else
#
# seed resolves KEY against the repos and reports the branch this machine is NOT
# on as INERT instead of as NO VALUE — an empty key there is the correct state,
# and printing it beside a genuine hole is how a report stops being read. The two
# lines below mirror, literally, the guard every service boots with:
# `env.REDIS_IS_CLUSTER !== '1' && !env.REDIS_URL`, in each service's `src/index.mts`.
REDIS_IS_CLUSTER=1
#
#! active-when REDIS_IS_CLUSTER=1 REDIS_DB1_HOST REDIS_DB2_HOST REDIS_DB3_HOST REDIS_DB1_PORT REDIS_DB2_PORT REDIS_DB3_PORT
REDIS_DB1_HOST=db1
REDIS_DB2_HOST=db2
REDIS_DB3_HOST=db3
REDIS_DB1_PORT=6379
REDIS_DB2_PORT=6379
REDIS_DB3_PORT=6379
#
# Read on both branches — a namespace is not a topology.
REDIS_KEY=marketplaceDev:
#
# Read ONLY on the single-node branch (REDIS_IS_CLUSTER not '1'), where it is
# the only thing naming the server. Left empty here it is inert and each repo
# keeps its own — which is the state this workspace runs in.
#! active-unless REDIS_IS_CLUSTER=1 REDIS_URL
REDIS_URL=
#
# 🔒 SECRET — the cluster's credentials, one pair for the whole platform.
#
# ⚠️ `marketplace-docker-DBs` carries a REDIS_PASSWORD of its own and it is NOT this one. There it is
# the `requirepass` written into the redis.conf that `./up.sh --with-redis` generates — an input to
# creating a container, not a credential for reaching the cluster. The two are never both live: that
# container puts the platform on the single-node branch, where the password travels inside REDIS_URL
# and REDIS_PASSWORD is not read at all. So seed sees two values and asks which repo to take it from;
# the answer is the services, unless you have actually moved onto the docker Redis. Whichever you
# pick, strip leaves the docker repo's own line alone — it reports it as KEPT — because up.sh
# requires it and reads it from that file.
REDIS_USERNAME=
REDIS_PASSWORD=


# ── MongoDB: the dev cluster (9 repos) ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 🔒 SECRET — carries the user and password in the URI.
# The eight services that touch the database plus marketplace-db-setup point at
# one replica set and one database. `yarn test:integration` ignores this and
# rebuilds a URL from the MONGO_TEST_* block below.
MONGODB_URI=


# ── Field encryption, CSFLE (9 repos) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠️ CSFLE_MASTER_KEY_PATH names a 96-byte file that must be THE SAME FILE on
# every service and on marketplace-db-setup — the data keys in the vault are
# encrypted under it, so a different key turns every encrypted field on the
# platform into an undecryptable blob. There is no escrow and no reset. Keep the
# file outside the repos, mode 400. This is the single strongest argument for
# the whole layer: it was the value most able to drift and the most expensive to
# get wrong.
CSFLE_MASTER_KEY_PATH=
#
# `<the database MONGODB_URI points at>.__keyVault` — the SAME database, never a
# vault database of its own: every user on this cluster is scoped to one
# database, so a vault elsewhere answers `Unauthorized` on the first createIndex.
CSFLE_KEY_VAULT_NAMESPACE=dbMarketplaceDev.__keyVault


# ── Cookie signing (7 repos) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 🔒 SECRET — the key-encryption key the Keygrip secrets are decrypted with.
# Its length rule lives in `readKek` and nowhere else, on purpose. Every service
# that validates a signed cookie needs the identical value or it rejects
# sessions minted by its neighbours.
KEYGRIP_KEK=


# ── Uploads (8 repos) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One directory on this machine, written by the resource services and read by
# the frontends' dev servers. An absolute path.
UPLOAD_DIR=/media/xxx


# ── Integration-test cluster accounts (11 repos) ──────────────────────────────
#
# 🔒 SECRET (the two passwords). Two users, the same split production uses:
# OWNER has dbOwner and replays the migrations, R/W has readWrite and is what
# the code under test connects with, so a resolver that quietly needs more than
# readWrite fails in the suite instead of in production.
#
# ⚠️ These four are shared, and the three keys that NAME the test database are
# not — MONGO_TEST_DB, MONGO_TEST_AUTH_ADMIN and MONGO_TEST_CONN_STRING stay in
# each repo because every suite drops its own database and no two may collide.
# The users are created per database with a role on it (see
# marketplace-db-setup/setup/mongodb.js) but carry one name and one password
# throughout. A machine that gives each test database a password of its own
# moves these four back into the repos — all four, not some.
MONGO_TEST_UDBOWNER=marketplaceOwnerTest
MONGO_TEST_PWDDBOWNER=
MONGO_TEST_UDBRW=marketplaceRwTest
MONGO_TEST_PWDDBRW=


# ── Error reporting (9 repos) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One Bugsink project for the whole platform. Empty or missing leaves the Sentry
# SDK inert and every service still boots — it is deliberately absent from every
# service's REQUIRED_ENV_VARS, so that a missing credential for the thing that
# REPORTS failures can never itself become one.
DSN=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT STAYS BEHIND, and why — each of these looks shared and is not.
#
# PORT                     nine distinct values; that is the point of nine
#                          services.
# DOMAIN                   `xxx.tdweb.lan` in the services, `127.0.0.1` in
#                          marketplace-services-status. One disagreement is
#                          enough: nothing here can be overridden.
# NODE_ENV                 a per-process mode, not a shared resource. Exporting
#                          it into every shell in the workspace reaches `yarn`,
#                          `vite` and `tsc` as well as the services, which is a
#                          far wider blast radius than the problem this file
#                          solves.
# QODANA_TOKEN             one token per Qodana Cloud project, and the projects
#                          are per repo.
# MONGO_TEST_DB            the throwaway database each suite drops. Sharing it
# MONGO_TEST_AUTH_ADMIN    would have two suites drop each other's data, in
# MONGO_TEST_CONN_STRING   parallel, with no error.
# SOCKETLABS_*             read by one service, the public resource one.
# APP_DOMAIN(_USER),       likewise one service. A key one repo reads has
# EMAIL_FROM, PLATFORM_NAME,  nothing to reconcile and belongs where it is read.
# STATIC_FOLDER, TURNSTILE_SECRET, KEYGRIP_KEY_*
# VITE_*                   frontend build-time values, one frontend each.
#
# ⚠️ THE COMMITTED `env` TEMPLATES KEEP EVERY KEY, including the ones above this
# block. They answer "what does this service read", which is a different
# question from "where does the value come from" — and a service's own template
# is the only place the first question has a complete answer. It is the real
# `.env` files the migration strips. `./scripts/env-diff.sh` knows the
# difference and reports a stripped key as `shared`, not as MISSING.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
